  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nCurrent-Tuesday... East central Florida remains wedged between high \npressure over the eastern U.S. and broad low pressure developing \nnear the Bahamas. A period of gusty northeast winds continues. Early \nafternoon observations have shown sustained winds around 20 mph \nwith gusts near 35 mph reaching inland locations, and have opted \nto extend the Wind Advisory along the coast to now include all of \neast central Florida except for Lake county. The Wind Advisory \nwill remain in effect for inland locations through 8PM this \nevening while continuing along the coast through 8AM Tuesday. Will\nhave to monitor trends in the wind forecast for Tuesday, with \nprobabilistic guidance suggesting areas south of the Cape as the \nbest candidates for any additional Wind Advisories. \n\nA period of astronomical high tides coincident with elevated onshore \nwinds and long wave periods will continue numerous coastal hazards. \nLife-threatening rip currents, rough surf, and large breaking waves \n8-13 ft are present at the local beaches, and entering the ocean is \nstrongly discouraged. Tidal observations at Trident Pier showed a \npreliminary crest to 3.02 ft (MHHW Datum) during this morning's high \ntide cycle, reaching Moderate Flood Stage. This crest represents the \nhighest non-tropical cyclone-related water level since the \nstation was established in October 1994. A Coastal Flood Warning \ncontinues into Tuesday with P-ETSS guidance showing a similar \ncrest to Moderate Flood Stage during the Tuesday morning high tide\ncycle. Salt water flooding peaking during the Tuesday morning \nhigh tide cycle will impact low-lying roads, lots, docks, and \nyards near the coast and intracoastal waterway. Pounding surf will\nalso continue Moderate to Major beach erosion at times of high \ntide.\n\nRounds of isolated to scattered onshore-moving showers continue \nearly this afternoon. A drier airmass advects across northern \nportions of the area late in the day, and this should gradually \nreduce shower coverage north of the Cape and the Orlando Metro \ntonight. The best chances for quick moving showers on Tuesday look \nto be focused across Okeechobee and the Treasure Coast (40-60%), but \ncan't rule out isolated showers (~20%) across coastal areas further \nnorthward. Lightning potential is low, remaining less than 20%. \n\nTemperatures remain seasonal early this week with highs in the low \n80s across much of east central Florida. Morning low temperatures \nare forecast in the low 70s for many areas, becoming slightly cooler \nin the upper 60s across far interior locations. \n\nWednesday-Sunday (previous)... High pressure across the eastern U.S. \nbecomes reinforced, but does show a weakening trend late in the \nperiod as it is pushed into the western Altc with approach of a \ndeveloping low pressure system. Breezy/gusty conditions continue \ninto late work-week as the pgrad remains rather tight. Poor to \nhazardous coastal/marine conditions continue alongside the period of \nhigh astronomical tide cycles. Highest PoPs occur Wed/Thu (esp Cape \nsouthward) during this period as another surge of moisture is poised \nto move across the region. Near to slightly above normal \ntemperatures are forecast with highs in the L-M80s. Lows continue \nabove climo well into the 60s to L70s at least thru Sat morning.",
